    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "The school canteen"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "The canteen records how many idlis, vadas and dosas were sold on 3 days. Writing the numbers in rows and columns makes a compact table - a matrix."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "matrix",
          "order m x n",
          "types of matrices",
          "equal matrices",
          "add same order",
          "scalar multiplication",
          "AB defined"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "A matrix is a rectangular arrangement of numbers in rows and columns, written in brackets.\nA matrix with m rows and n columns has order m x n, read m by n.\nSpecial types: row matrix (one row), column matrix (one column), square matrix (m = n), zero matrix, diagonal, identity (1 on the main diagonal, 0 elsewhere).\nTwo matrices are equal if they have the same order and equal corresponding entries.\nTo add or subtract matrices they must have the same order; we add or subtract corresponding entries.\nTo multiply by a scalar k, multiply every entry by k.\nTo multiply matrix A (m x n) by B (n x p), the number of columns of A must equal the number of rows of B; the product is m x p. Each entry is row times column."
        }
      },
